h=A.d([A.h(d,d,A.q(d,d,d,d,d,d,d,d,d,d,d,d,d,d,d,d,d,!0,d,d,d,d,d,d,d,d),"Located at the end of Parliament Street, the Parliament House, also known as Sansad Bhawan, is a magnificent circular colonnaded building where the representatives of Indian democracy gather. This iconic structure serves as the seat of India's democratic institutions, where crucial discussions and debates about the country's future take place.\n\n\nOriginally designed for the Chamber of Princes, the Council of State, and the Legislative Assembly, the building now houses the Lok Sabha (House of the People), Rajya Sabha (Upper House), and the Library. Parliament House was designed by renowned architects Sir Edwin Lutyens and Sir Herbert Baker. The foundation stone was laid by H.R.H. the Duke of Connaught on February 12, 1921, and after about six years of construction, it was inaugurated by Lord Irwin, the then Governor-General of India, on January 18, 1927.\n\n\nInitially planned as an extension of the Viceroy\u2019s House (Rashtrapati Bhavan), the building was re-designated as the Parliament House following the Montague-Chelmsford reforms of 1919. It is a symbol of Indian democracy, featuring a 29.9-meter-high dome topped with the Indian flag. Spanning 2.02 hectares, the building has a diameter of 170.69 meters.\n\n\nThe structure is surrounded by a colonnade with 144 identical columns, each 8.23 meters high. It is enclosed by an ornamental red sandstone wall or iron grills. The building combines classical Indian art with modern scientific techniques, as seen in its design, decorations, and layout, which includes gardens, paved lanes, ornate buildings, and beautifully sculptured statues.\n\n\nVisitors wishing to view the parliamentary sessions in the public gallery must obtain a permit from the reception office on Raisina Road, New Delhi. Since buses are not allowed near the Parliament House, taxis or auto rickshaws are the best means of transport to reach the site.\n")],t.R)
