h=A.d([A.h(d,d,A.q(d,d,d,d,d,d,d,d,d,d,d,d,d,d,d,d,d,!0,d,d,d,d,d,d,d,d),'Raj Ghat is a solemn memorial dedicated to Mahatma Gandhi, the Father of the Nation and a pivotal figure in India\'s struggle for independence. Located on the banks of the Yamuna River, it consists of a simple black marble platform that marks the spot where Gandhiji was cremated on January 31, 1948, following his assassination. An eternal flame burns near the platform, symbolizing the ongoing spirit of his teachings. A stone footpath leads to the memorial, and the area is surrounded by beautiful lawns and fountains, offering a peaceful environment for reflection.\n\n\nThe memorial bears the poignant epitaph "He Ram", which were the last words spoken by Gandhi before his death, meaning "O Ram" or "O God." Visitors are required to remove their footwear as a sign of respect before approaching the memorial. Every Friday, a commemorative ceremony is held to mark the day of Gandhi\'s death, and prayer sessions take place on his birth and death anniversaries.\n\n\nWithin the Raj Ghat complex, visitors will also find the memorials of other prominent Indian leaders, including Jawaharlal Nehru, Indira Gandhi, Sanjay Gandhi, Rajiv Gandhi, and Lal Bahadur Shastri. Two museums dedicated to Gandhiji are located nearby, housing various belongings, photographs, and exhibits related to his life and legacy.\n\n\nRaj Ghat was designed by Vanu G. Bhuta and has received several awards for its architectural significance. It is customary for foreign dignitaries visiting India to pay their respects at the site by laying wreaths on the platform.\n')],t.R)
