c=A.d([A.h(a,a,A.q(a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,!0,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a),'Garba Raas is an ancient and beloved dance form that has been a cornerstone of Gujarat\u2019s cultural celebrations. Traditionally, it was popularized by Usha, the granddaughter-in-law of Lord Krishna, and was originally known as Lasya Nritya. Unlike Dandiya Raas, Garba Raas is performed exclusively by women. The dance is typically performed to honor Ma Jagdambe, the Mother Goddess, during festivals like Navratri, Sharad Purnima, Holi, and Vasant Panchami.\n\nIn Garba, women dress in colorful traditional attire and perform rhythmic circular movements, often carrying earthen pots (known as garbas) on their heads. These pots are sometimes filled with water or an oil lamp is lit inside them. The word "Garba" itself is derived from the term Garbhadip, meaning a lamp inside an earthen pot, symbolizing life and creation. The performers move gracefully in circles, clapping and twirling to the beats of devotional songs sung in praise of the Goddess. The lively rhythm and beautiful choreography create a festive atmosphere that unites the dancers and spectators alike in a joyous celebration of devotion.')],i)
