g=A.d([A.h(a0,a0,A.f().$0(),"Gir Forest National Park is perhaps the most famous wildlife reserve in Gujarat and one of the most iconic wildlife destinations in India. Located in the Saurashtra Peninsula, about 65 km southeast of Junagadh, it is the only natural habitat of the Asiatic lion outside Africa. Established in 1965, the park covers a vast area of 1412 sq. km, with 258 sq. km designated as the national park and the rest as a sanctuary. The park was originally created to protect the dwindling population of Asiatic lions, which now thrive in the region.\n\nThe Gir Forest is characterized by its rugged terrain, steep hills, deep valleys, and numerous mountain streams. The ecosystem here includes grasslands, moist deciduous forests, and semi-arid landscapes, making it home to a wide variety of species. Apart from the Asiatic lions, the sanctuary is home to 32 mammal species, including leopards, hyenas, wild boar, and the Indian gazelle. Over 310 bird species and 24 reptile species can also be spotted in the park.\n\nLion Safari is the most popular activity in the park, with jeep safaris offering tourists the chance to spot the majestic lions along with other wildlife. Visitors can also enjoy birdwatching and explore the diverse flora and fauna of this unique ecosystem.")],j)
