g=A.d([A.h(a0,a0,A.q(a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,!0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0),"Leaning Temple of Huma\nOne of Sambalpur's most intriguing attractions is the Leaning Temple of Huma, devoted to Lord Shiva. True to its name, the temple leans mysteriously to one side. Located on the rocky banks of the Mahanadi River, this architectural marvel was built by the Chauhan kings, who were known for their splendid constructions. The cause of its tilt remains a mystery, adding an enigmatic allure to this sacred site.\n\nNrusinghanath Temple\nHidden in the lush greenery of Gandhamardan Hills, Nrusinghanath Temple is another jewel of Sambalpur. This ancient temple is dedicated to Lord Marjara Kesari, believed to be a re-incarnation of Lord Vishnu. Surrounded by stunning waterfalls and verdant forests, the temple exudes peace and spirituality. The sacred Papaharini stream flows nearby, forming five pristine pools that hold great significance for the locals.\n\nHirakud Dam\nA modern engineering marvel, Hirakud Dam is the longest dam in the world and a must-visit attraction in Sambalpur. Constructed in 1957, this massive structure was built to control floods and support irrigation. Visitors can enjoy breathtaking views from the Gandhi Minar at the northern end and the Jawahar Minar at the southern end. A bike ride near the dam is an exhilarating way to explore its grandeur.\nDebrigarh Wildlife Sanctuary\nThe deciduous forests of Debrigarh Wildlife Sanctuary, located near the emerald waters of Hirakud Dam, are a paradise for nature lovers and wildlife enthusiasts. This sanctuary is home to diverse flora and fauna, including dry deciduous trees like Sal and Dhaura. During the monsoon, the area comes alive with cascading waterfalls and lush greenery.")],j)
