j=t.R
g=A.d([A.h(a,a,A.q(a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,!0,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a),"Kumbhalgarh Fort\n Kumbhalgarh Fort, an architectural marvel of Rajasthan, was built by Rana Kumbha in the 15th century. The fort is surrounded by a 36 km long wall with seven majestic gates and ramparts, making it one of the largest forts in the region. The fort offers a panoramic view of the hills and forests, and within its walls, you'll find the Rana Kumbha palace, Badal Mahal, and over 360 Hindu and Jain temples. Don't miss the Lord Shiva temple, Neelkanth Mahadeo. The fort is open daily from 9:00 am to 6:00 pm.\nBadal Mahal\n The Badal Mahal, or Palace of the Clouds, is the highest structure in Kumbhalgarh Fort. This beautiful palace offers stunning views of the surrounding countryside and the fort. It is one of the most picturesque sites inside the fort, providing a perfect spot for photography and sightseeing.\nKumbhalgarh Wildlife Sanctuary\n Spanning 586 sq km, the Kumbhalgarh Wildlife Sanctuary is home to diverse wildlife, including panthers, sloth bears, four-horned antelopes, and crocodiles. The sanctuary also attracts migratory birds in winter, making it a haven for nature lovers and wildlife enthusiasts. The best time to visit for a safari is between December and March.\nMucchal Mahavir Temple\n Located 5 km from Ghanerao in the Kumbhalgarh Sanctuary, the Mucchal Mahavir Temple is famous for its statue of Lord Mahavira, adorned with a mustache. The temple is surrounded by statues of elephants, adding to its grandeur. Nearby Garasia tribal villages also draw many visitors to this site.\nParshuram Temple\n The Parshuram Temple, located in a cave, was once used by Rishi Parshuram for meditation. Inside, visitors will find shrines dedicated to Lord Shiva and Lord Ganesha, along with nine holy kunds that never dry up. It's a serene and spiritual spot for visitors seeking tranquility.\nMammadev Temple\n Situated at the base of the Kumbhalgarh Fort, the Mammadev Temple was built by Rana Kumbha in 1460. Known for its intricate sculptures, the temple houses the cenotaphs of Rana Kumbha and Prithviraj Chauhan. It's a must-visit for history and architecture enthusiasts.\nKumbhalgarh Festival (December)\n The Kumbhalgarh Festival, held every December, turns the fort into a vibrant spectacle with yellow lights and decorations. The three-day celebration features games like Tug of War and Musical Chair for children, along with thrilling dance performances by local artists, such as Kalbeliyas, Langas, Terahtaali, Kachchi Ghodi, and Odissi dancers.")],j)
