j=t.R
g=A.d([A.h(a,a,A.q(a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,!0,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a),"Lake Palace and Lake Pichola\n The Lake Palace, a stunning structure made of marble and granite, sits on Lake Pichola. It is known for its splendid pavilions, courtyards, and hanging gardens, offering a picturesque view of the serene waters. The palace is also historically significant, with marble porticos used for weighing Mewar sovereigns in gold, which was later distributed to the poor.\nBhim Vilas Palace\n Part of the City Palace complex, the Bhim Vilas Palace is famous for its elaborate wall paintings, with the Mor Chowk being particularly known for its beautiful peacock mosaics.\nJag Niwas and Jag Mandir\n Jag Niwas, now a luxury hotel, was once the summer residence of Mewar's princes. Nearby, Jag Mandir is an island palace with a marble dome and red sandstone construction, once serving as a refuge for Mughal Emperor Shah Jahan during his exile.\nCity Palace and Museum\n The City Palace is the largest palace complex in Rajasthan, overlooking Lake Pichola. It is a grand amalgamation of several buildings, offering courtyards, pavilions, and gardens. The City Palace Museum showcases artifacts and artwork from the royal Mewar dynasty.\nSaheliyon-Ki-Bari\n Situated to the north of Udaipur, this small but beautiful garden was built for the maids of honor. It features fountains, lotus pools, kiosks, and marble elephants, making it a tranquil spot for visitors.\nJagdish Temple\n Built in 1651, the Jagdish Temple is the largest temple in Udaipur. Its Indo-Aryan architecture houses a black stone image of Lord Vishnu and a brass image of Garuda.\nBhartiya Lok Kala Mandir\n The Indian folk arts museum, Bhartiya Lok Kala Mandir, exhibits folk dresses, musical instruments, dolls, and paintings. Visitors can also enjoy daily puppet shows that highlight traditional Rajasthani culture.\nAhar\n Located just 3 km from Udaipur, Ahar is known for its cenotaphs commemorating Mewar's royalty. It was once the capital of the Sisodia dynasty.\nRanakpur\n Ranakpur is famous for its beautifully carved Jain temples, showcasing intricate architecture and design.\nSajjangarh\n The Sajjangarh Palace, also known as the Monsoon Palace, is located on a hilltop and provides a panoramic view of the surrounding Mewar countryside. Initially built as an astronomical center, it later became a hunting lodge and a monsoon palace.\nEklingji\n Located about 22 km from Udaipur, the Eklingji Temple is a two-story marble structure dedicated to Lord Shiva. It is a significant religious site set in a deep ravine with a serene atmosphere.\nNathdwara\n Nathdwara, a famous Hindu pilgrimage site located 48 km from Udaipur, is known for its Pichwai paintings and the temple of Lord Krishna.\nNagda\n Nagda features ancient temples, such as the Adbhutji Temple and the Sas-Bahu Temples, which date back to the 11th century.\nChittorgarh\n Chittorgarh, the historical capital of the Sisodia dynasty, is home to the majestic Chittorgarh Fort. Visitors can explore the fort and nearby attractions like Kumbhalgarh, Rajsamand Lake, and Haldighati.")],j)
