g=A.d([A.h(a0,a0,A.q(a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,!0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0),"Sculptures\nThe museum's collection of sculptures is particularly noteworthy, with significant pieces originating from Udaipur, Pilak, and Jolaibari. The sculptures from Pilak are especially famous for depicting a blend of Hinduism and Buddhism. Dating back to the 9th to 13th centuries, these sandstone sculptures are celebrated for their artistic style and variety.\n\nTerracotta Figures\nThe museum also boasts an impressive collection of terracotta figures from Pilak and Amarpur, many of which belong to the Bengal School of art. The collection was curated by the famous historian Sri Dinesh Ch. Sen and provides valuable insight into the culture, devotion, and beliefs of the people of ancient \n\nTripura.\nBronze Images\nAmong the most captivating exhibits are the bronze images, which showcase the fine art of moulding and sculpting. These include miniatures of various deities, such as Mukhalinga Avalokitesvara, Vishnu, and Tara. The intricacy of the craftsmanship on these images adds to their historical and artistic value.\n\nCoins\nThe museum also houses a rare collection of coins from the reign of Maharaja Ratna Manikya, the 145th king of Tripura. These coins, made from gold, silver, and copper, are dated back to 1464 A.D. and are valuable for researchers and heritage enthusiasts alike.\n\nPaintings\nA highlight of the museum's collection is the impressive array of life-size portraits of former rulers of Tripura. The most significant contributions to the museum\u2019s art collection come from renowned artists such as Dhiren Krishna Deb Barma, Nandalal Bose, and Gagan Thakur. These paintings offer a glimpse into the royal history and artistic legacy of the region.")],j)
