j=A.d([A.h(e,e,A.q(e,e,e,e,e,e,e,e,e,e,e,e,e,e,e,e,e,!0,e,e,e,e,e,e,e,e),'The name Sarnath, derived from "Saranganatha" or "Lord of the Deer," traces back to the area\u2019s early names: Mrigadava (the Deer Park) and Isipatana (the sacred place where holy men descended from heaven). Unfortunately, the city\'s monasteries, stupas, and structures suffered considerable damage during the 12th-century Turkish invasions. In the 19th century, British archaeologist Alexander Cunningham excavated the ruins of Sarnath, revealing much of its ancient history. Even today, ongoing excavations by the Archaeological Survey of India, led by BR Mani, continue to uncover artifacts and sculptures from the Gupta period.\n\nAmong the key attractions in Sarnath is the Ashoka Pillar, erected by Emperor Ashoka in the 3rd century BCE. This pillar stands 15.24 meters tall and is crowned with the famous Lion Capital, which is now India\u2019s national emblem. Other significant sites include the Dhamek Stupa, a prominent Buddhist shrine marking the location of Buddha\'s first sermon, and the Sarnath Museum, which houses an impressive collection of artifacts, including the Lion Capital and sculptures from the Gupta period.\n\nThe Chaukhandi Stupa, which dates back to the 5th century, is another historical landmark. It marks the place where Buddha met his first disciples and was later modified during the Mughal era to commemorate Emperor Humayun\u2019s visit. For those interested in exploring more, nearby excursions to Varanasi, Chunar Village, and Bodh Gaya are recommended.\n\nShopping in Sarnath offers a variety of religious and cultural items, including images and statues of Buddha, as well as silk robes, devotional items like prayer wheels, rugs, beads, and flags. The best time to visit is from October to March, with the Buddha Purnima Festival in April-May being another great time to experience the city.\nTo reach Sarnath, you can fly into Varanasi\'s Lal Bahadur Shastri Airport, which is 24 km away, or travel by train to Varanasi Junction, which is just 6 km from the site. Buses also run from Varanasi, and local rickshaws and auto-rickshaws are available for short-distance travel within the city.\n\nSarnath offers a profound and enriching experience, perfect for those interested in history, spirituality, and the growth of ancient civilizations. Exploring this city will not only teach you about its past but also leave a lasting impact on your own journey.')],t.R)
