g=A.d([A.h(a,a,A.q(a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,!0,a,a,a,a,a,a,a,a),'Himachal Pradesh is a stunning state located in the Indian Himalayas, known for its lush meadows, sprawling valleys, snow-covered peaks, serene lakes, and rivers. It has a number of hill stations that attract tourists from all over the world, especially during the summer months when people flock to escape the heat of the plains. Some key hill stations in Himachal Pradesh include:\nShimla: Known as the "Abode of Goddess Shyamala," Shimla is a charming hill station perched at 2159 meters above sea level. Major attractions include the Mall Road, Vice-Regal Lodge, and Christ Church, along with panoramic views of the surrounding mountain ranges.\n\n\nManali: Situated at 2050 meters, Manali is often referred to as the "Valley of the Gods." Its popular sites include the Hidimba Temple, Gadhan Thekchhokling Gompa, and the Museum of Traditional Himachal Culture, along with opportunities for adventure sports.\n\n\nDharamshala: Located in the Kangra Valley, Dharamshala is known for its Tibetan culture, particularly the Namgyal Monastery and the Tsuglagkhang temple, home to the Dalai Lama. The Kangra Art Museum and Nechung Monastery are other notable attractions.\n\n\nDalhousie: Sprawled over five hills, Dalhousie offers tranquility and natural beauty. Key attractions include the Kalatope viewpoint, Panchpulla, and Dainkund (Singing Hill), along with a glimpse into India\u2019s freedom struggle.\n\n\nKhajjiar: Known as the "Mini Switzerland of India," Khajjiar is a picturesque meadow surrounded by deodar trees and a 12th-century Khajinag temple. It also offers a nine-hole golf course and a small pond with floating islands.\n\n\nLahaul Spiti: This rugged region, often called "Little Tibet," is known for its ancient monasteries like the Kyi Gompa, trekking destinations like Chandratal Lake, and scenic views of the Spiti Valley.')],j)
