i=A.j("Places to See in Mussoorie",a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,A.e(j,h,a0,i,a0,14,A.b().gh().gi().x,B.a,0,a0,!1),a0,a0,a0,a0)
h=A.p(a2,a0,r).w.gk()
j=t.R
g=A.d([A.h(a0,a0,A.q(a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,!0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0,a0),'Gun Hill\nGun Hill is the second highest peak in Mussoorie, offering stunning views of the surrounding Himalayan ranges. It is named after the gun that was once fired from the top to help people adjust their watches. Visitors can reach the hilltop via a cable car that offers thrilling views of Mussoorie and the Doon Valley.\n\nMunicipal Garden\nLocated about 3 km from the Library Point, the Municipal Garden is a delightful picnic spot featuring an artificial mini lake with boating facilities. The garden is home to over 800 species of flowers and attracts nature lovers for its rich flora and fauna.\n\nChilder\'s Lodge\nChilder\'s Lodge, located at the highest point in Mussoorie (Lal Tibba), offers breathtaking views of the sunrise and sunset. The lodge is accessible on foot or by horse ride and provides a peaceful spot for nature lovers to enjoy the scenic beauty of the area.\n\nCamel\'s Back Road\nThis 3 km stretch between Kulri Bazaar and Library Bazaar is lined with deodar trees and offers panoramic views of the snow-capped mountains. The road is named after a rock formation resembling the back of a camel and is a perfect spot for morning and evening walks.\n\nJharipani Fall\nLocated 7 km from Mussoorie on the Dehradun-Musoorie road, Jharipani Falls is a picturesque spot surrounded by lush greenery. The falls offer beautiful views of the Shivalik ranges and attract nature lovers and trekkers alike.\n\nBhatta Fall\nBhatta Falls, situated 7 km from Mussoorie on the Mussoorie-Dehradun road, is a peaceful spot with a small stream and a pond for swimming. It is an excellent picnic spot, especially for families.\n\nKempty Falls\nKempty Falls, located 15 km from Mussoorie, is a popular tourist destination where water cascades from an altitude of 4,500 feet. The British used to host tea parties here, which is why the falls are named "Kempty" (from "Camp-tea"). The falls are one of the most visited spots in the region.\n\nNag Devta Temple\nNag Devta Temple, located about 6 km from Mussoorie, is an ancient temple dedicated to Lord Nag Devta. The temple offers scenic views of the Doon Valley and Mussoorie town.\n\nMussoorie Lake\nMussoorie Lake, located 6 km from Mussoorie, is a man-made lake surrounded by greenery. Visitors can enjoy boating here and marvel at the enchanting views of the Doon Valley and surrounding villages.\n\nVan Chetna Kendra\nThis is a popular picnic spot situated 3 km from Mussoorie on the Tehri Bypass road. The area is covered with pine trees and colorful shrubs, making it a charming place to enjoy nature. The area is also home to exotic wildlife species like the Himalayan Peacock.\n\nSir George Everest House\nLocated 6 km from Mussoorie, the Sir George Everest House was the residence of Sir George Everest, the first Surveyor General of India. The house offers panoramic views of the Doon Valley and the snow-capped Himalayan ranges.\n\nJwalaji Temple\nSituated 9 km from Mussoorie on top of Benog Hill, Jwalaji Temple is dedicated to Mata Durga. The temple offers stunning views of the Himalayas, Doon Valley, and Yamuna Valley, and is surrounded by dense forests.\n\nClouds End\nBuilt in 1838, Clouds End is one of the first four buildings of Mussoorie. It is now a resort offering panoramic views of the snow-capped Himalayas and is a popular destination for honeymooners and foreign tourists.')],j)
