f=A.d([A.h(a4,a4,A.f().$1$fontSize(12),"The origins of Durga Puja lie in the tale of Mahishasura (Mahisha meaning buffalo and Asura meaning demon), a mighty demon who wreaked havoc across the three worlds\u2014heaven, earth, and the netherworld. Empowered by a boon from Lord Shiva, Mahishasura believed he was invincible, as no man, god, or beast could kill him. However, his arrogance overlooked the power of women.\n\nTo end his reign of terror, Lord Vishnu led the gods in a grand yajna, summoning an extraordinary woman of unparalleled courage and strength. From this divine ceremony emerged Goddess Durga, radiant with beauty and charged with supreme energy. Armed with the most potent weapons gifted by the gods, she confronted Mahishasura in a fierce battle. After an epic struggle, she vanquished him, proving the might of women and establishing the triumph of good over evil.\n\nGoddess Durga, the embodiment of Shakti (power), is depicted as a ten-armed warrior riding a roaring lion, her trident piercing Mahishasura's chest. This iconic image symbolizes victory over tyranny and darkness.")],h)
